        "summary": [
          {
            "subjectId": "prop",
            "points": [
              "A compressed rota cut burnout by a fifth with no harm to throughput.",
              "Rest days that cannot be cancelled matter more than shorter hours.",
              "Turnover at 14% and replacement costs four months' salary.",
              "A shorter standard week is not untried: the 1931 Act phased in a two-day weekend.",
              "Adopt means legislate a phased timetable, not switch overnight; put trials in the statute."
            ]
          },
          {
            "subjectId": "opp",
            "points": [
              "Pilots are knowledge-work; evidence is silent on hospitals, transport, care.",
              "A nationwide mandate redistributes labour: hire at large cost or compress and stretch the queue.",
              "Holding coverage constant costs 18–27% more staffed hours, roughly a tenth on total.",
              "Only two of nine recovered part of it through sickness and turnover, measured badly.",
              "Wider coverage standards cost real money; continuity is the scope; trials first, not a national standard now."
            ]
          }
        ],
        "keyDisagreement": "Whether to adopt a nationwide four-day standard now, when the extra staffing cost is documented but offsetting savings remain unmeasured.",
